<BlogPost title="Editorial Policy" description="How Colombia Force works: sources, scope and corrections." pubDate={new Date('2026-09-23')}>
  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>Colombia Force is an independent digital news outlet. It is not owned by, or affiliated with, the Government of Colombia or any official body. We summarize, in plain language, public information about operations, seizures and results of the offensive against crime.</p>
  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p>We rely on official statements and accounts from the Presidency, the Ministry of Defense, the Armed Forces, the National Police and the Attorney General's Office. Every article links to its original source.</p>
  <h2>Scope and limits</h2>
  <p>Our coverage focuses on the government's security actions and their results. We do not aim to cover every aspect of the conflict or of Colombian politics. The figures we publish are those reported by the authorities and have not always been independently verified, so we always attribute them to their source.</p>
  <h2>Standards</h2>
  <p>People who are arrested or named are described as alleged offenders until a judge rules otherwise. We do not publish graphic or explicit images.</p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you spot an error, write to us through the <a href="/en/contact">contact page</a>. We correct mistakes visibly and state what was changed.</p>
</BlogPost>
